  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "A", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 1.* The only reactions in the syllabus are combustion and substitution by a halogen; bromine water and permanganate leave them unchanged.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "C", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 2.* One bromine atom adds to each carbon of the double bond, giving BrCH₂CH₂Br, 1,2-dibromoethane.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "C", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 3.* Liver enzymes oxidise ethanol to ethanal and then to ethanoic acid, which is why the products accumulate in the blood.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "D", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 4.* Joining an acid CₙH₂ₙO₂ and an alcohol and losing water gives CₙH₂ₙO₂ for the ester.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "A", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 5.* A diet with enough energy but too little protein causes kwashiorkor, with oedema and a swollen belly.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "B", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 6.* Silver chloride is the white precipitate; nitric acid prevents a carbonate precipitate from being mistaken for it.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "B", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 1.* Lubricating oil is C₂₀–C₅₀, heavier than diesel (C₁₄–C₂₀) and kerosene (C₁₀–C₁₄).
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "C", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 2.* Cold dilute permanganate gives the diol; hot concentrated permanganate would cleave the bond to acids.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "D", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 3.* Zymase is the enzyme complex in yeast that converts glucose to ethanol and carbon dioxide.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "A", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 4.* One end loves water and the other fears it, which is exactly what a surfactant needs.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "C", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 5.* Benedict's solution must be warmed or boiled for the colour to appear.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "D", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 6.* A pure solid melts sharply at one temperature; a range of six degrees indicates an impurity.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "A", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 1.* A pure substance boils at one fixed temperature; a mixture boils, and condenses, over a range.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "B", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 2.* Poly(styrene) is rigid and transparent, and expanded polystyrene is the familiar white foam.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "B", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 3.* Ethanoic acid is an acid, so it liberates CO₂ from a carbonate, and it esterifies with ethanol.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "C", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 4.* One mole of fat gives three moles of soap; M ≈ 306, so 0.300 × 306 = 91.8 g.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "A", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 5.* Glucose is stored as glycogen in the liver and muscles; any excess beyond that is converted to fat.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "D", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 6.* c = n/V with V in dm³, so c = n × 1000/V(cm³).
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "B", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 1.* Two miscible liquids with different boiling points are separated by fractional distillation.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "A", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 2.* Full hydrogenation adds two molecules of hydrogen to the triple bond, giving the saturated alkane.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "D", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 3.* Both are hydrogen-bonded, so they are completely miscible.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "C", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 4.* Esterification removes water and hydrolysis adds it back, splitting the ester into acid and alcohol.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "B", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 5.* Sucrose is the standard non-reducing sugar because both anomeric carbons are joined.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "C", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 6.* Barium gives an apple-green flame; calcium is brick-red and copper blue-green.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "D", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 1.* Both are C₄H₁₀ with different skeletons; the other pairs have different molecular formulae.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "A", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 2.* The double bond starts at carbon 2 when numbered from the nearer end, so it is but-2-ene.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "A", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 3.* Dilute ethanoic acid is vinegar, used to preserve and flavour food; the pure acid is a chemical feedstock.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "D", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 4.* The methyl group of the alcohol and the ethanoate group of the acid give methyl ethanoate.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "B", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 5.* Table sugar is sucrose, extracted from cane or beet.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "C", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 6.* n(NaOH) = 2.50 × 10⁻³ mol, the ratio is one to one, so c(HCl) = 2.50 × 10⁻³ × 1000/20.0 = 0.125 mol dm⁻³.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "C", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 1.* Boiling range rises with chain length, and refinery gas has the shortest chains of all the fractions.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "B", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 2.* The acid removes water from the alcohol — dehydration — and leaves the double bond.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "A", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 3.* Ethane-1,2-diol lowers the freezing point of water and raises its boiling point, which is exactly what a coolant must do.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "D", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 4.* The resin is heavily cross-linked and cannot be remelted; the thermoplastics can.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "A", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 5.* Iodine detects the starch, and Benedict's the reducing sugar; they are separate tests on separate portions.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "C", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 6.* Calcium hydroxide is slightly soluble and may not precipitate from dilute solution; the flame test identifies it.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "B", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 1.* Kerosene is less dense than water and immiscible with it, so it forms the upper layer.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "D", dense: true, pad: 5.5pt)[
    *Chapter 2.* Addition polymerisation loses no atom, so the mass of polymer equals the mass of monomer used.
